AB  - Aflatoxin contamination of cacao beans is caused by environmental factors like climate change and temperature fluctuation. A variety of image processing, including color, can determine aflatoxin detection in cacao bean quality. The utilization of a microcontroller for its cost, portability, and capacity for image processing, the Raspberry Pi may be used in this field to detect aflatoxin through image acquisition. The researcher designed a portable device that implements the detection of aflatoxin in cacao by utilizing OpenCV image processing, integrating the Raspberry Pi for preprocessing, extraction, and classification with computer vision, and actual detection of aflatoxin in the cacao bean. The device captures images and produces accurate findings using an image-processing ANN machine learning and SVM algorithm. Applying this enhances the detection process in real-time for the cacao bean. The component captures an image of the cacao bean based on the color, providing its image classification of aflatoxin and non-aflatoxin. The investigation needs an in-depth assessment of the accuracy and reliability of the detection method. Utilizing the confusion matrix, an accuracy of 80.49 % was achieved, where 482 beans were trained and tested for classification into two categories: those containing aflatoxin and those without. Image processing, with its various methods, can adapt image detection to classify the specified aflatoxin in the cacao bean.
