AB  - Background and Objectives: Cacao ( Theobroma cacao L.) supports millions of Latin American smallholders. In Peru, over one hundred thousand families rely on this crop, with the Piura region widely recognized for its fine-flavor local variety, "Blanco de Piura." However, regional productivity is constrained by low yields. This study evaluated yield performance, its key driving factors, and the profitability of soil amendments and mineral fertilization in cacao, following the preliminary validation of a short-term harvest estimation index. Methods The field trial was conducted in a cacao agroforestry system over two years using a completely randomized design with nine treatments: control, full and half doses of mineral fertilizer, biochar, yeast, humisil, and their combinations. The harvest index (HI) was validated with the actual harvest. In addition, predictive modelling and a cost-benefit analysis were performed across all treatments. Results The HI was validated (R 2 = 0.71). Although yield differences were not statistically significant, mean yield increased by approximately 29% with mineral fertilization, ~ 26% with yeast plus biochar, and ~ 25% with biochar plus half-dose fertilizer; conversely, humisil combined with fertilizer reduced yield by ~ 11% relative to the control. The individual applications of biochar and mineral fertilizer were the primary factors positively influencing yield performance. Under baseline experimental market conditions, the control was the most profitable treatment; however, net financial gains were realized for the other treatments under the 2024 price peak and in 2025. Conclusions These findings highlight the importance of evaluating profitability alongside crop yield in field trials to justify their adoption by regional farmers.
