AB  - Introduction: Cocoa mucilage (Theobroma cacao L.) has evolved from being considered a processing residue to being recognized as a raw material with potential for the development of foods, fermented beverages, and value-added ingredients. This study analyzed the evolution of scientific production, thematic structure, food applications, and the main research trends related to its utilization. Methodology: A documentary, descriptive, and interpretative study was conducted, integrating bibliometric analysis and qualitative thematic review. Records were retrieved from Scopus and Web of Science on July 13, 2026. Of the 436 documents initially identified, 127 duplicates were removed. Subsequently, Q1-Q2 journal selection criteria and thematic relevance assessment were applied, resulting in a final corpus of 214 articles published between 1966 and 2026 across 131 scientific sources. Results: Scientific production showed an annual growth rate of 4.08%, an average of 33.49 citations per document, and 36.45% international collaboration. The thematic map identified motor themes related to cocoa pulp, beverages, enzymes, and bioactive compounds; basic themes associated with fermentation and microorganisms; niche themes linked to aroma compounds and biomass valorization; and emerging or declining themes related to cocoa sweatings, sensory evaluation, and new biotechnological pathways. Applications included wines, beers, kefir, probiotic juices, kombucha, syrups, sorbets, and natural sweeteners. Conclusions: The evidence reveals a transition toward the valorization of cocoa mucilage as a food raw material. Its industrial consolidation requires further advances in standardization, food safety, stability, sensory acceptance, economic feasibility, and sustainable scale-up.
