AB  - Amazonian tropical soils are characterized by high acidity, intense weathering, and low natural fertility, making nutrient dynamics essential for the sustainability of land-use systems. This study evaluated macro- and micronutrient dynamics and soil chemical properties in organic cocoa agroforestry systems and secondary forest areas in the Brazilian Amazon. Twelve land-use systems were evaluated in Pará State, Brazil, including ten organic cocoa agroforestry systems and two secondary forest areas distributed across the Tomé-Açu and Transamazon regions. Soil samples were collected at four depths (0–10, 10–20, 20–30, and 30–40 cm) and analyzed for macronutrients, micronutrients (copper, zinc, manganese, and iron), potential acidity, sum of bases, cation exchange capacity, and pH. Data were analyzed using analysis of variance, Tukey’s test (p < 0.05), principal component analysis, hierarchical cluster analysis, Spearman’s correlation, and linear regression. Older agroforestry systems, particularly RT-AFS5 in the Transamazon region and TA-AFS5 in the Tomé-Açu region, exhibited higher concentrations of Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, K⁺, zinc, manganese, sum of bases, and cation exchange capacity, reflecting enhanced nutrient cycling and organic matter accumulation. Principal component analysis explained 72.1% of the total variability, indicating a clear soil fertility gradient, whereas hierarchical cluster analysis showed that system similarity was more strongly associated with soil chemical characteristics and management practices than with system age alone. Linear regression indicated progressive nutrient accumulation with increasing system age. Overall, organic cocoa agroforestry systems demonstrated the potential to improve soil chemical quality, supporting ecological restoration and sustainable soil management in the Amazon.
