AB  - Much of the world’s remaining tropical forest occurs outside formally protected areas and is instead located within privately managed landscapes. Understanding how different forms of agricultural land use influence forest loss on private properties is, therefore, critical for evaluating conservation strategies that rely on production landscapes. Shade-grown cacao agroforestry systems in Brazil’s Atlantic Forest are widely promoted as conservation-compatible land uses that integrate agricultural production with tree cover. In southern Bahia, traditional cabruca systems cultivate cacao beneath a partially retained forest canopy, yet the relationship between various shade-grown cacao agroforestry systems and property-level deforestation remains unclear. This thesis evaluates whether rural private properties containing shaded cacao agroforestry experienced different deforestation outcomes than properties without shaded cacao between 2018 and 2023. I conducted a spatial analysis of deforestation trends using the MapBiomas annual land-cover data on 117,185 properties, with property boundaries derived from the Cadastro Ambiental Rural (CAR). Properties were classified as containing shaded cacao or not using the MapBiomas Cacau dataset (2018–2020 reference period). Deforestation was quantified as the percentage of each property’s area that experienced forest loss during the study period. I compared deforestation occurrence and intensity using descriptive statistics and Welch two-sample t-tests. I also estimated associations using ordinary least squares regression with log10-transformed deforestation and logistic regression models predicting the probability of any deforestation, while controlling for property size, accessibility, slope, and elevation. During the study period, 42.7% of properties experienced forest loss. Deforestation prevalence was statistically significantly higher on shaded cacao properties (50.4%) than on non-shaded cacao properties (29.7%), as was mean percent deforestation (8.34% versus 3.64%, respectively). In iii adjusted models, shaded cacao presence remained statistically significantly positively associated with forest loss, corresponding to approximately fourfold higher deforestation intensity after back-transformation. Logistic regression results indicated that shaded cacao properties had 2.4 times higher odds of experiencing any deforestation. These findings indicate that shaded cacao presence does not correspond with reduced deforestation risk at the property scale in southern Bahia. This pattern likely reflects a combination of factors, including active canopy management within cacao agroforestry systems and broader landscape dynamics, as properties located farther from roads were associated with higher deforestation in the regression results. While shaded cacao systems may retain structural complexity and support biodiversity relative to more intensive agricultural land uses, these results suggest that their presence alone does not guarantee reduced forest loss. More broadly, the findings highlight the importance of distinguishing between biodiversity benefits within agroforestry systems and landscape-scale deforestation dynamics when evaluating agroforestry as a conservation strategy in fragmented tropical forests
