AB  - Cadmium (Cd) concentrations in cacao‐growing soils may vary according to soil properties, soil depth, and spatial distribution. The objective of this study was to evaluate the association of soil pH and organic matter (OM) with Cd concentrations at two soil depths in Loreto and San Martín, Peru. A total of 520 samples corresponding to 260 plots were analyzed. Differences between soil depths were evaluated using paired Wilcoxon signed‐rank tests, while adjusted associations were assessed using linear mixed‐effects models. Spatial distribution was evaluated using ordinary kriging. In Loreto, Cd concentrations were significantly higher at 0–30 cm than at 30–60 cm, whereas no significant differences between depths were detected in San Martín. In the mixed‐effects model, soil pH and OM were positively associated with Cd concentration, corresponding to estimated increases of 15.1% per one‐unit increase in pH and 6.3% per one‐percentage‐point increase in OM, respectively. Cross‐validation R 2 values for the geostatistical models ranged from 0.15 to 0.42 in Loreto and from 0.16 to 0.41 in San Martín, indicating variable and generally modest predictive performance. Overall, the results show that Cd concentrations in cacao‐growing soils are associated with soil pH, OM, and regionally heterogeneous spatial patterns; however, these associations do not allow inferences regarding Cd mobility, bioavailability, or transfer to cacao plants or beans.
